  "worked_solution": "Let $S$ be the event that the selected student is engaged in a sports section, and let $B$ be the event that the student is a boy. Then ${B}^{\u2032}$ is the event that the student is a girl.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student is engaged in a sports section, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S\\cap B)+P(S\\cap {B}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nThere are $20$ boys in the class, and $5%$ of them are engaged in a sports section. So, the number of boys engaged in a sports section is\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{100}\\cdot 20=1.\n$$\n\nThere are $24$  girls in the class, and $25%$ of them are engaged in a sports section. So, the number of girls  engaged in a sports section is\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{100}\\cdot 24=6.\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(S\\cap B),$ we take the number of boys engaged in a sports section $(1)$ and divide it by the total number of students in the class $(20+24=44).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap B)=\\frac{1}{44}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(S\\cap {B}^{\u2032}),$ we take the number of girls engaged in a sports section $(6)$ and divide it by the total number of students in the class $(44).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap {B}^{\u2032})=\\frac{6}{44}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected student is engaged in a sports section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S\\cap B)+P(S\\cap {B}^{\u2032}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{44}+\\frac{6}{44} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{44}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
